<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglo-Burmese Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1824">1824</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anglo-Burmese Wars were three wars fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and the Konbaung kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in the nineteenth century. The First (1824–1826) cost Burma Arakan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a> and its claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and Manipur; the Second (1852–1853) gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>; and the Third (1885) ended the monarchy, after which all of Burma was annexed to British India in 1886.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglo-burmese-wars.jpg" alt="Anglo-Burmese Wars" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Willoughby Wallace Hooper · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Third_anglo-burmese_war.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Anglo-Burmese Wars, also called the Indo-Burmese Wars, were an armed conflict between two expanding empires, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and the Konbaung dynasty. The expansion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, present-day Myanmar, under the Konbaung dynasty had consequences along its frontiers. As those frontiers moved ever closer to the British East India Company and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, there were problems both with refugees and with military operations spilling over ill-defined borders. Three wars were fought between 1824 and 1885.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a> (1824–1826) ended in a victory for the British East India Company. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-yandabo">Treaty of Yandabo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> lost territory it had conquered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, Manipur and Arakan. The British also took possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a>, intending to use it as a bargaining chip in future negotiations with Burma or Siam. As the century wore on, the Company began to covet the resources and main part of Burma during an era of great territorial expansion.</p><p>In 1852 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a> sent Commodore Lambert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> over a number of minor issues arising from the earlier treaty. The Burmese at once made concessions, including the removal of a governor whom the British had made their casus belli. Lambert eventually provoked a naval confrontation in extremely questionable circumstances, which began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-burmese-war">Second Anglo-Burmese War</a> in 1852. It ended with the British annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> province, renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>, and led to a palace revolution in which King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagan-min">Pagan Min</a> (1846–1853) was replaced by his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">Mindon Min</a> (1853–1878).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindon-min">King Mindon</a> tried to modernise the Burmese state and economy to secure its independence and established a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, which he fortified. The efforts proved unsuccessful. The British claimed that Mindon's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thibaw-min">Thibaw Min</a>, who ruled from 1878 to 1885, was a tyrant intending to side with the French, that he had lost control of the country and so allowed disorder on the frontiers, and that he was reneging on a treaty signed by his father.</p><p>They declared war again in 1885 and conquered the rest of the country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-burmese-war">Third Anglo-Burmese War</a>, which ended in the total annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. The wars became the longest and most expensive of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>'s wars, costing between 5 and 13 million pounds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pound-sterling">sterling</a> over more than 60 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anglo-Burmese Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
